# The committed fixtures are compared byte for byte against what the generator
# produces. Without this, git applies CRLF conversion to them on a Windows
# checkout and the PDFs are corrupted — silently, and only for some clones.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ndjson -text

# uv.lock is deliberately NOT marked -diff. A lockfile diff is noisy, but it is
# also the place an unexpected dependency change shows up, and hiding it would
# remove the one review surface where that is visible.

# The console's source is hashed byte for byte, so a bundle built from anything
# other than the committed source fails the suite (docs/adr/0003). A digest
# taken over LF on one clone and CRLF on another would fail for everybody except
# whoever last built it — the same silent, per-clone breakage as above. So the
# source is LF everywhere, and the built bundle is never converted at all.
console/** text eol=lf
src/rcm_agent/console/static/** -text

# The published example run is an artifact, not source. A Review names its
# Determination by a digest over the exact bytes of `claims/<id>.json`
# (ADR-0004), so a checkout that rewrote its line endings would make the
# committed digests wrong on exactly the machines that convert them.
docs/example-run/** -text
